<div class="hero-lead"><p class="hero-kicker">Inheritance conflict</p><h1>Family access is still access when trust breaks.</h1><p>An estate dispute can change the meaning of arrangements made years earlier. Help with an account, a shared cloud folder or a family recovery contact may still connect someone else to your private correspondence or financial information.</p><p><a class="btn" href="#contact">Request a private conversation</a></p><p class="trust">In person &#xB7; With you present &#xB7; You control your credentials</p><p>It may be hard to separate practical help from arrangements you now want to reconsider. Dr. Lior Tabansky works with you, in person, to understand the connections in your own digital environment and explain your technical options. You decide what to change.</p></div></div></section><nav class="page-index wrap" aria-label="On this page" data-presentation-ui><a href="#access-shared-in-good-faith-can-still-matter">Your situation</a><a href="#work-through-your-own-access-with-expert-help">First paid visit</a><a href="#contact">Contact</a></nav>
<section class="copy-section" id="access-shared-in-good-faith-can-still-matter" aria-labelledby="access-shared-in-good-faith-can-still-matter-heading"><div class="wrap copy-body"><div class="section-title"><h2 id="access-shared-in-good-faith-can-still-matter-heading">Access shared in good faith can still matter</h2></div><div class="section-detail">
<p>Help with an account or a shared family service can connect several parts of your digital life. The effects may extend beyond the arrangement you remember making.</p>
<p>Dr. Tabansky examines the relevant connections within your authorized environment and agreed scope, including access to private data and the arrangements you depend on to manage your accounts. You gain an explanation of what remains connected and what separating it would mean.</p>
<p>Remaining access does not establish that a relative has used it or acted improperly.</p>
</div></div></section>
<section class="copy-section" id="understand-the-connections-before-changing-them" aria-labelledby="understand-the-connections-before-changing-them-heading"><div class="wrap copy-body"><div class="section-title"><h2 id="understand-the-connections-before-changing-them-heading">Understand the connections before changing them</h2></div><div class="section-detail">
<p>Changing one password does not tell you what a shared service or recovery setting allows. Ending an arrangement can also affect access you rely on.</p>
<p>We explain the relevant connections and the effects of proposed changes. You decide what to authorize. The work concerns access you are authorized to manage; it does not decide ownership or legal entitlement to family or estate information.</p>
</div></div></section>
<section class="copy-section copy-offer" id="work-through-your-own-access-with-expert-help" aria-labelledby="work-through-your-own-access-with-expert-help-heading"><div class="wrap copy-body"><div class="section-title"><h2 id="work-through-your-own-access-with-expert-help-heading">Work through your own access with expert help</h2><p><strong>Private Exposure Assessment &#xB7; Typically four hours</strong></p><p class="offer-price"><strong>&#x20AA;3,600 including VAT</strong></p></div><div class="section-detail">


<p>The paid visit includes inspection of relevant access on one main phone and one PC, iPad or second phone, including digital identities, accounts, apps, recovery routes and access to data. We complete the agreed intake, access mapping, diagnosis and approved urgent first aid where safely possible within scope.</p>
<p>You receive a practical summary of what was reviewed, found and changed, what remains and the next steps, even if you buy nothing further. We review only what is disclosed, observable or otherwise lawfully accessible within your authorized environment and agreed scope. Additional work or price requires your agreement first.</p>
